# awcms-astro — configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Everything down to the last section is read at
# BUILD time; nothing in this file reaches the browser. Astro only inlines
# variables prefixed `PUBLIC_`, and none of these are — do not add a `PUBLIC_`
# alias for the token.

# --- Site identity -----------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical absolute origin. Canonical links, hreflang alternates, Open
# Graph URLs, robots.txt, and the sitemap are all built from it. A wrong value
# does not fail the build; it publishes a site pointing every crawler elsewhere.
SITE_URL=https://example.com
SITE_NAME=Example Site
SITE_DESCRIPTION=Public information site built on the awcms-astro template.

# Optional glyph shown before the site name in the header. Empty means no
# glyph, which is the default and is a complete, styled state.
#
# Do NOT put a state emblem, official logo, or institutional insignia here. A
# site built from this template is independent, and an emblem in its header
# contradicts that in one glance — see AGENTS.md §Keamanan. It is decoration
# only: it renders `aria-hidden`, so nothing here reaches a screen reader.
SITE_MARK=

# Optional Open Graph / Twitter share card. Absolute URL, or a path to a file
# you have actually placed in `public/` (e.g. `/og-image.png`).
#
# Leave it EMPTY unless the file exists. Empty is a supported state: the pages
# then emit no image tag at all and social previews fall back to a clean text
# card. Pointing this at a file that is not there is worse than leaving it
# unset — the preview breaks, the JSON-LD claims a 1200×630 image that is not
# served, and nothing in the build says so. This template ships no card
# generator; see README, "Yang belum ada".
#
# The card should be PNG at 1200×630. Those dimensions are what the page
# declares, and a mismatch makes some scrapers letterbox or reject the card.
SITE_SOCIAL_IMAGE=

# How many article cards one section page carries. Default 12; a whole number
# above zero, and a malformed value FAILS the build rather than being ignored.
#
# PRD FR-DSC-006 asks for a bounded archive before production volume. Without a
# bound a section renders its entire history into one document, which for the
# 23,906-article migration target is every headline the newsroom has ever
# published in a single HTML response. Page 1 stays at `/<section>/`; pages
# 2..N live at `/<section>/halaman/N/`.
SITE_POSTS_PER_PAGE=12

# Locale prefixes OTHER than the default locale, comma separated. Must match
# `localeMeta` in src/config/site.ts — astro.config.mjs cannot import that file
# (it runs first), so the two are kept in step by hand.
SITE_LOCALES=en

# --- awcms content backend ---------------------------------------------------
# Origin of the awcms instance. No trailing slash needed; one is stripped.
#
# Since ADR-0043 this value is read TWICE, and the second reading makes it
# reader-visible:
#
#   1. the build calls it with the machine credential below, as it always has;
#   2. its ORIGIN is rendered into /cari/ and named in the `connect-src`
#      directive, so the reader's browser calls it directly for search.
#
# Consequences of getting it wrong, in the order you will meet them:
#
#   - unset, or not an http(s) URL → the build has no CMS at all. There is no
#     search box on the site (deliberately: a box that cannot answer is worse
#     than no box), and `connect-src` stays `'self'`.
#   - set to an origin the reader's browser cannot reach (a private address, a
#     hostname that only resolves inside your network) → the site builds
#     perfectly and every search in a reader's browser fails. Nothing on your
#     side reports it.
#   - set to an origin that IS reachable but where this site's public domain is
#     not a registered, verified, active tenant domain → awcms answers the
#     neutral empty payload with no CORS grant, which is byte-identical to "no
#     results". The search box then finds nothing, for every query, silently.
#     Register the site's origin in awcms's tenant domains (its ADR-0107).
AWCMS_API_URL=https://cms.example.com

# --- Visitor beacon (ADR-0044) ------------------------------------------------
# The tenant code awcms needs in a page-view beacon's body, and the ONLY switch
# that turns the beacon on. Empty (the default) means this site sends nothing.
#
# It is NOT the retired AWCMS_TENANT_CODE, which THROWS if you set it. That one
# used to select which tenant the build published, and could silently disagree
# with the token. This one selects nothing: it is the public code that already
# appears in awcms's own public URLs, and it cannot change what this site builds.
#
# There is deliberately no separate on/off flag. A switch and a value that must
# both be set is a pair that gets half-set, and the half-set state here is a
# site reporting page views into whichever tenant a stale code happens to name.
#
# What the beacon does and does not do (ADR-0044, Option B):
#
#   - the call carries NO credentials, so awcms's 30-day `awcms_visitor_key`
#     cookie is discarded by the browser and nothing persistent lands on the
#     reader's device. That is why this site owes readers no consent banner;
#   - page views are counted, unique visitors are NOT. Every view looks like a
#     first visit, and "12,000 views" is not convertible into "how many people";
#   - it needs AWCMS_API_URL, because it posts to that same origin and that
#     origin is what `connect-src` names.
#
# BEFORE YOU FILL THIS IN, check one thing this repo CANNOT check for you:
# whether that awcms tenant has visitor-analytics `rawIpEnabled` switched on. It
# is off by default over there and stores readers' IP ADDRESSES rather than only
# a salted hash. ADR-0044 refuses this declaration while it is on, and nothing
# here can see it — only you can.
SITE_BEACON_TENANT_CODE=

# Whether this site publishes a newsletter subscription form (ADR-0049).
#
# `true` turns on three surfaces at once: the form in the site footer, the page
# at /newsletter/confirm, and the page at /newsletter/unsubscribe. Those two
# paths are NOT this repo's to rename — awcms builds the links it emails from
# them, and a renamed page breaks a link that is already in somebody's inbox.
#
# It takes effect only when AWCMS_API_URL is set: all three are calls to awcms
# from a reader's browser, and a declaration with no origin behind it would
# publish three surfaces that can do nothing.
#
# What this build CANNOT check, and does not pretend to: whether the awcms
# tenant has the `newsletter` module enabled. A disabled module answers the same
# neutral body as a successful subscription — awcms ADR-0103's anti-enumeration
# decision working as designed — so a site can publish a form that quietly
# collects nothing. That check belongs on the CMS's own admin screen, where the
# person who can act on it is looking.
#
# Turning it on also adds a section to the privacy page, because an email
# address is the first per-person data this site asks a reader for.
SITE_NEWSLETTER=

# A MACHINE CREDENTIAL, and the only variable that names a tenant.
#
# Shape: awcmsm_<32 hex tenant id>_<43 char secret>. Issue it from awcms's
# /admin/machine-credentials screen (since 13 August 2026; the same surface is
# still reachable as POST /api/v1/access/machine-credentials), and set
# `allowed_permission_keys` to exactly
# ["blog_content.posts.read", "media_library.media.read"] — nothing wider.
#
# The plaintext is shown ONCE, in the issuance response. Reloading that page
# spends a credential which then has to be revoked. Revocation lives on the same
# screen, which is the reason it was built: a leaked token is not the moment to
# be remembering an API call.
#
# Issue it against a service account belonging to THIS SITE'S tenant. A machine
# credential inherits its service account's `principal_kind`, and a delegated
# actor — an agency reaching into a customer tenant — is refused
# `403 PARTNER_SUSPENDED` at every request the day that partnership is
# suspended, while the grant that conferred the access stays in place (awcms
# ADR-0093). The build then fails exactly like a revoked token.
#
# The media key is not optional and not for images alone: `bun run build` ends
# in `scripts/asal-media.mjs`, which asks awcms for its public media origin so
# the server can emit an `img-src` that admits it. Without the key that call is
# a 403 and the BUILD FAILS — after every page has already rendered, which makes
# it read like a broken deployment rather than a missing permission. A
# deployment with no public media still needs it; the answer it writes is then
# `configured: false`.
#
# THIS token cannot change anything, because it is issued with no write actions
# at all. Say it that way round: until 13 August 2026 "a machine credential
# cannot write" was a property of the CLASS, and awcms ADR-0092 ended that. A
# write-class credential now exists — actions capped in code to `create`/`update`,
# CIDR-bound, refused outright when the caller's IP is unknown, and capped at 30
# days instead of a year. Every credential issued before that migration stayed
# read-only, and this one must STAY read-only: that is now an issuing decision to
# defend, not a guarantee to inherit. Issue it with no `allowed_write_actions`.
#
# It CAN still read this tenant's content, which is why the credential expires
# and why revocation takes effect on the next request.
#
# A human session token will not work here, and that is deliberate on the awcms
# side: sessions expire, a password reset revokes every session of that
# identity, and an MFA step-up rotates the token. Any of those would kill a
# build at a moment nobody can predict, far from the cause.
#
# The token carries its own tenant, so no tenant header is sent and none is
# honoured. See docs/adr/0018-*.md.
#
# ISSUE IT FROM awcms'S DEFAULT (OWNER) TENANT — the same tenant awcms resolves
# as its PLATFORM tenant (docs/adr/0022-*.md). Until 2 August 2026 awcms could
# only ever have one tenant, so this was not a choice; awcms ADR-0054 made a
# second tenant possible, and the question now has more than one answer.
#
# You can see which tenant that is: awcms's /admin/tenants screen marks it with
# a `platform` badge, and its uuid is on the same row. That row is also where
# AWCMS_TENANT_ID below comes from.
AWCMS_API_TOKEN=awcmsm_replace-me

# OPTIONAL. How long one request to awcms may take before the build gives up,
# in milliseconds. Default 30000.
#
# It is deliberately generous: `view=full` pages carry whole posts including
# contentJson, and a large tenant on a cold database can legitimately take many
# seconds. Tuning this down to a "healthy request path" value turns a slow build
# into a failed one, which is the opposite of what it is for.
#
# What it exists to catch is not slowness but SILENCE — a connection that is
# accepted and never answered, which is how a database out of connections
# usually fails. `fetch` has no default deadline, so without this a build hangs
# until the CI job's own limit kills it (with a message naming the job, not
# awcms) or, on a workstation, forever.
#
# A value that is not a positive number is REFUSED, not ignored — including `0`,
# which looks like "no limit" and would restore exactly the hang above.
# AWCMS_API_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# --- Tenant assertion --------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL, and strongly recommended: the tenant uuid this site is SUPPOSED to
# publish. It does not select anything — the token above already did that. What
# it does is fail the build when the two disagree.
#
# That is the mistake worth catching. Nobody can "guess" a tenant anymore, but
# anybody can paste the staging token into the production deployment, and that
# failure does not look like one: the build stays green, the site fills up, and
# the articles belong to somebody else. Stating the tenant here turns that into
# a build error naming both ids.
#
# Copy it from the `platform`-badged row of awcms's /admin/tenants screen — the
# tenant this site is supposed to publish (docs/adr/0022-*.md). Guessing it
# defeats the point: an assertion that happens to match a wrong token is not a
# check, it is a coincidence.
#
# AWCMS_TENANT_CODE and AWCMS_DEFAULT_TENANT_CODE are GONE. They are refused
# rather than ignored — a value that reads like configuration and decides
# nothing is worse than no value at all.
AWCMS_TENANT_ID=

# --- Runtime (the only variables read AFTER the build) -----------------------
# Since ADR-0016 the built site is served by a Bun process (`bun run serve`, and
# `bun dist/server/penyaji.mjs` inside the image) instead of nginx. These two are
# the only variables that process reads — it never reads an `AWCMS_*` variable,
# because a finished build never contacts awcms again.
#
# The defaults are what the container needs, so a Coolify deployment sets
# neither. Override them only when something else already owns port 8080.
#
# HOST defaults to 0.0.0.0 because the process runs inside a container behind
# Traefik: one that listens on localhost only is unreachable from outside the
# container, and that failure shows up as a health check that fails for no
# stated reason. On a workstation it means the preview is reachable from your
# whole network — set HOST=127.0.0.1 if that matters to you.
# PORT=8080
# HOST=0.0.0.0
